  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nLate evening KDDC WSR-88D shows considerably less showers across\nsouthwest Kansas just before midnight. The shower and storm\nactivity that impacted us earlier has moved well to the east\nacross eastern Kansas. With that being said, we are watching the\nnext slug of showers that are moving across the southern High \nPlains. This activity may impact southwest Kansas later in the \nmorning, although confidence is not high on this. Speaking of \nconfidence, a general perusal of 00Z CAM data shows considerable\ndisagreement in shower evolution over the next 18 or so hours. \nDespite this uncertainty, examining the atmosphere still shows a\nvery moist profile. Our 00Z RAOB measured a PWAT of 1.62\". \nLooking at SPC PWAT climo, this is still well above the daily \nmax of 1.34\". The normal PWAT for this time of year is at a much\nlower 0.77\". Considering this, it should still be easy for the \natmosphere to squeeze out shower activity. Therefore, putting \nthis all together, will continue with the fairly high pops for \nthis afternoon. A mid level trof will be in close proximity to \nwestern Kansas this afternoon, so logic would state that there \nshould be an west to east pop gradient, as this mid level \nfeature start to clear the FA. Indeed, the NBM pops are \nadvertising this, which looks meteorologically cohesive. The \nmain threat with this activity is moderate to heavy rainfall. \nWPC has us in a marginal risk in the Excessive Rainfall Outlook \nfor today, with the higher probabilities shifted east across the\nFA. Otherwise, outside of this widely scattered activity, a \ngenerally drier forecast is expected for the remaining FA.\n\nBoth deterministic models as well as their ensemble counterparts\nare forecasting shortwave ridging developing across the greater\nregion over the weekend. This would support the notion of a \ndrier and warmer forecast. Highs for Saturday and Sunday should \npeak in the lower to mid 80s. Lows should be fairly mild, and \nlikely to be above normal with values only bottoming out in the \nmix of 50s to 60s. \n\nBy Monday, we should be in between weather systems with the main\nfocus this morning will be afternoon convection and fog/low \nclouds this morning. Highest probability for early morning \nstratus with ceilings below 500 ft AGL will be in the Hays and \nGarden City area through at least 15z with ceilings then \ngradually improving through 18z. Low clouds will also being \npossible in the Liberal and Dodge City area with cloud bases \nforecast to be between 500 and 1000ft AGL. Areas of fog this \nmorning will reduce visibilities as low as 2 miles at times. \nCeilings will gradually improve through the day with MVFR \nconditions likely this afternoon and VFR conditions being \npossible by late day/early evening. Thunderstorm chances will \nimprove this afternoon across southwest Kansas with the best \nopportunity for the steadier and heavier storms occurring at \nLiberal and Garden City between 18z and 23z today and then Dodge\nCity between 21z today and 02z Saturday. he shortwave ridge \nwell east of the region. Long range models and their ensemble \ncounterparts then show for the possibility of a large trof to \nstart to carve out across the southwestern United States. In \naddition, remnant tropical disturbance moisture might mix in, \nfurther complicating the synoptic picture. Examining both GEFs \nand ENS output, it is very unclear how much these two \ndisturbances will interact with each other and where exactly the\nstorm track will be. The NBM does continue to ramp up pops over\nthe middle portion of the business week to account for the \npossibility of more unsettled weather. This is something to \nwatch as there is essentially very little agreement in the \nensembles.",
